<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · Protest and freedom · 1908 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Rota</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Polish song of 1908, &quot;The Oath&quot;, written against German rule and its suppression of Polish culture.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Maria Konopnicka</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Feliks Nowowiejski</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Poles under the partitions; the Polish independence movement</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Cieszyn (words); Kraków, Poland (first sung)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words 1908, first published 7 November 1908; music 1910, first sung 15 July 1910</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics by Maria Konopnicka, first published 7 November 1908; music by Feliks Nowowiejski, 1910)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>Maria Konopnicka, a poet and activist for Polish independence, wrote &quot;Rota&quot; (&quot;The Oath&quot;) in 1908 while staying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Cieszyn</a>, as a protest against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>'s policy of forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanisation</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> in the lands <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> had annexed in the partitions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. Under Prussian and then German rule there, political leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> and Eugen von Puttkammer had pressed for the &quot;uprooting&quot; of the Poles, and the poem was written as a reply. It was first published in the newspaper Gwiazdka Cieszyńska on 7 November 1908.</p><p>Feliks Nowowiejski, a composer, conductor and organist, set it to music two years later. It was first sung in public in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> on 15 July 1910, at a patriotic demonstration marking the 500th anniversary of the Polish-Lithuanian victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> at Grunwald, and it quickly became popular across partitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Until 1918 it was the anthem of the Polish Scouting movement. From 1920 to 1922 it was the state anthem of the Republic of Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, a puppet state wholly dependent on interwar Poland, and in Poland itself it was among the songs considered for the national anthem, favoured especially by the political right; &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland-is-not-yet-lost">Poland Is Not Yet Lost</a>&quot; was chosen instead.</p><p>During the German occupation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, on the eve of Independence Day, 11 November 1939, scouts in Zielonka near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> put up posters bearing the poem; in reprisal the occupiers executed nine scouts and other inhabitants of the town. The communist authorities after the war also kept the song, making it the anthem of the 1st <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadeusz-kosciuszko">Tadeusz Kościuszko</a> Infantry Division. After 1989 it became the official anthem of the Polish People's Party and later of the League of Polish Families. Its melody was played by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> carillon until 2003 and served as the signature theme of two regional television stations; in 2010 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> honoured the song and its author by a special resolution, and since May 2024 an autograph score of 1910 has been on permanent display in the Palace of the Commonwealth in Warsaw.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A solemn oath of loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and its culture, sworn in defiance of forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanisation</a>. Its mood is grave and resolute.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Polish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Polish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Nie rzucim ziemi, skąd nasz ród,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We will not abandon the land our people come from,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Nie damy pogrześć mowy!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We will not let our speech be buried!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Polski my naród, polski lud,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We are the Polish nation, the Polish people,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Królewski szczep piastowy,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of the royal stock of Piast,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Nie damy, by nas zniemczył wróg…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We will not let the enemy Germanize us…</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">— Tak nam dopomóż Bóg!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>— So help us God!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Do krwi ostatniej kropli z żył</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To the last drop of blood in our veins</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Bronić będziemy Ducha,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We will defend the Spirit,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Aż się rozpadnie w proch i w pył</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Until into dust and ashes falls apart</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Krzyżacka zawierucha.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The storm of the Teutonic Knights.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Twierdzą nam będzie każdy próg…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Every threshold will be a fortress for us…</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">— Tak nam dopomóż Bóg!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>— So help us God!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Nie będzie Niemiec pluł nam w twarz,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The German will not spit in our face,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Ni dzieci nam germanił.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Nor Germanize our children.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Orężny wstanie hufiec nasz,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our armed host will rise,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Duch będzie nam hetmanił,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Spirit will be our hetman,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Pójdziem, gdy zabrzmi złoty róg…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We will go when the golden horn sounds…</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">— Tak nam dopomóż Bóg!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>— So help us God!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Maria Konopnicka (1908). Text from <a href="https://pl.wikisource.org/wiki/Rota_(Konopnicka)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (5)</summary><ul><li>line 1.4: 'szczep piastowy' = the stock (tribe) of the Piasts, the first royal dynasty of Poland.</li><li>line 2.4: 'Krzyżacka zawierucha' = the storm (turmoil) of the Krzyżacy, the Teutonic Knights; here a figure for Prussian and German rule.</li><li>line 2.3: 'w proch i w pył' = into dust and powder; 'ashes' is used for 'proch', which means dust (also gunpowder).</li><li>line 3.4: 'Duch będzie nam hetmanił' = the Spirit will lead us as hetman, the commander of the old Polish army; the verb is made from the noun.</li><li>line 3.5: the golden horn ('złoty róg') is the call to rise, as in Wyspiański's play The Wedding (1901).</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
